data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector. \n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=3-2.5x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-41738/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-41738"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-139618"] :question/problem "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $6$ | $4$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1.7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6-1.7x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-139646/selection/9c09df97700624afe42e951b7d94fc4012226e12d758c81fb7ebc14b3f0af859" :answer/id #uuid "1630c3fe-314c-5989-aaea-7c97ec9d0fed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2-6.25x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139646/selection/3e0c5656c76aa384bc67e57b4b2d574ede19e0ce2350a94f82b8540fce1a2b3b" :answer/id #uuid "d74c8c82-c7de-585e-9fe2-797eeb2dbebd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2-6.25x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139646/selection/5d0ea88187048a1ddc9792473be0587a40c2