s the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{56}[\\begin{aligned}14 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 28\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6.25 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y=6.25+2x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-139646/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139646"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139617/selection/e29d32780e4890cfe9d7fc7856f1edcc0e204a9aa1cb430838fb168aa633275b" :answer/id #uuid "801ce760-7e23-5f9a-ad15-99c8abf027b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2-1.4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139617/selection/8f41cdeabbca04fcb29bd47c3cf7a8aae6fec8611e742b09b355680cb6b4f713" :answer/id #uuid "1b8a42c6-f983-5775-bfca-21a05221218e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=1.4-2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139617/selection/d79800e348c79dbc87e805e6e442eb1df7eae2de140b96a370317ac2655c6103" :answer/id #uuid "9a6b41dd-5085-565c-bcc4-f1f87a7e36c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-2+1.4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139617/selection/7882382a38ee64d21b605b1736d6e1a147fd786ca343307f607a85de374a5d3c" :answer/id #uuid "f830982b-10a0-52af-89d7-1d958d7e6f20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=1.4+2x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139617/selection/93e298bec62d41121aa2094dad2e51c452be8139300974602153da5dad3ae128" :answer/id #uuid "c01cc395-1e94-5e8f-af96-f3e97f4de178" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2+1.4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139617/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6fdd51a-e92c-5013-bf78-c8f49f8d0960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139617/selection/93e298bec62d41121aa2094dad2e51c452be8139300974602153da5dad3ae128" :answer-field/choices ["q-139617/selection/e29d32780e4890cfe9d7fc7856f1edcc0e204a9aa1cb430838fb168aa633275b" "q-139617/selection/8f41cdeabbca04fcb29bd47c3cf7a8aae6fec8611e742b09b355680cb6b4f713" "q-139617/selection/d79800e348c79dbc87e805e6e442eb1df7eae2de140b96a370317ac2655c6103" "q-139617/selection/7882382a38ee64d21b605b1736d6e1a147fd786ca343307f607a85de374a5d3c" "q-139617/selection/93e298bec62d41121aa2094dad2e51c452be8139300974602153da5dad3ae128"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139617" :question/id #uuid "f59e05eb-a82f-5ff7-9613-77ece9768e07" :question/math-academy-id "q-139617" :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-2$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$1$** | **$2$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1.4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=2+1.4x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-139617/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "950c1ed7-f804-54f5-bf97-16bf2d1a663b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139617"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139736/selection/ead6153315666ad9fc9a72cd549813cea6cec97b4daaee21f004c8a1c99983b3" :answer/id #uuid "8554e9db-06be-5c81-8521-c98dea6b7335" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$906$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139736/selection/a9f357d11f3ec8669d9c6bac471edab90cb05da3501d210e3f067a39d7765266" :answer/id #uuid "fc036480-0155-5a05-af43-6f4aaf86f5da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$872$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139736/selection/c0536225002474d9302c87c2aca342eb14a6b05b5342d6275be2954b2aa42977" :answer/id #uuid "3f0c1fdf-8390-59e7-8b89-99f515b96225" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$868$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139736/selection/be28bf2c66036b21a663dfd84a90a4cfdfc47d58d59d60e5ebd61b69e2a67a5f" :answer/id #uuid "2dbe9689-b2b8-5838-b210-1b670233ec1e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$902$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139736/selection/2404173d2bc58e1164efcf4e0758fd08d792d09cae7a80e3187956044a056703" :answer/id #uuid "777ff542-a657-5249-be0a-93a1da42bf20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$826$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139736/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "10471fd5-efae-5858-aec5-7c6b12872d9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139736/selection/ead6153315666ad9fc9a72cd549813cea6cec97b4daaee21f004c8a1c99983b3" :answer-field/choices ["q-139736/selection/ead6153315666ad9fc9a72cd549813cea6cec97b4daaee21f004c8a1c99983b3" "q-139736/selection/a9f357d11f3ec8669d9c6bac471edab90cb05da3501d210e3f067a39d7765266" "q-139736/selection/c0536225002474d9302c87c2aca342eb14a6b05b5342d6275be2954b2aa42977" "q-139736/selection/be28bf2c66036b21a663dfd84a90a4cfdfc47d58d59d60e5ebd61b69e2a67a5f" "q-139736/selection/2404173d2bc58e1164efcf4e0758fd08d792d09cae7a80e3187956044a056703"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139736" :question/id #uuid "7a7db993-94b0-573c-8bae-99041a111b86" :question/math-academy-id "q-139736" :question/problem "A statistician collected data on the height above the ground (in hundreds of meters) of a hot air balloon during the first five minutes of its flight. This data is shown below. \n\n| Time $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.6$ | $3.0$ | $4.6$ | $6$ | $7.6$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the balloon's height after $6$ minutes, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1.6 \\\\ 3.0 \\\\ 4.6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 7.6\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}22.8 \\\\ 83.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.06 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.06+1.5x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the height of the balloon after $6$ minutes, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.06+1.5(6) \\\\  & =9.06\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the balloon's height after $6$ minutes will be $906$ meters.  \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-139736/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139736"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-139749/selection/ae485033e895c242c742fd7b4b80e24dd8c71fe545d14fc0ee9033c924ac1178" :answer/id #uuid "2135a0e6-7b97-5fff-bdd7-b1adf6e3127a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4.25$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139749/selection/90db0881a93774e7ee9b692ad7629a45161458253ff3f85b61d07bdbe04157d3" :answer/id #uuid "1d4aa237-3168-52c3-b900-5d4cc90dfa52" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3.25$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139749/selection/fbc12d36957286a21f33d2a30b6d2b3362d453b821d5b2bae784ef0dc31904b6" :answer/id #uuid "b40d0f96-3fdd-508a-84c1-73757c94203f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4.75$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139749/selection/7029ea74e6e3d10d94b12fd1390e67923b11e7a1348d2d04db2b123cdee3f561" :answer/id #uuid "a5d4ed2b-77d1-5f16-999e-6217a5829b22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5.15$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139749/selection/1a3c12f2d1440b69b9d47249fd5232655728e841bf226d54db07dbbf0cca211b" :answer/id #uuid "43af6b1f-c10c-5d7b-a095-813f288cee75" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3.75$ meters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139749/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "07dda353-44b6-532b-a9e9-8b95d1a31f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139749/selection/1a3c12f2d1440b69b9d47249fd5232655728e841bf226d54db07dbbf0cca211b" :answer-field/choices ["q-139749/selection/ae485033e895c242c742fd7b4b80e24dd8c71fe545d14fc0ee9033c924ac1178" "q-139749/selection/90db0881a93774e7ee9b692ad7629a45161458253ff3f85b61d07bdbe04157d3" "q-139749/selection/fbc12d36957286a21f33d2a30b6d2b3362d453b821d5b2bae784ef0dc31904b6" "q-139749/selection/7029ea74e6e3d10d94b12fd1390e67923b11e7a1348d2d04db2b123cdee3f561" "q-139749/selection/1a3c12f2d1440b69b9d47249fd5232655728e841bf226d54db07dbbf0cca211b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139749" :question/id #uuid "3ba26ac4-59dd-55c3-b644-94e5b96251c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-139749" :question/problem "A bungee jumping instructor measures how much a bungee rope stretches (in meters) when one end of the rope is attached to the bridge railing while a weight of $x$ kilograms is hung on the other. This data is shown below.\n\n| Weight $(x)$ | **$10$** | **$20$** | **$30$** | **$40$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the rope stretch if the instructor uses a $65$-kilogram mass, assuming the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{500}[\\begin{aligned}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 20 \\\\ 1 & 30 \\\\ 1 & 40\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{500}[\\begin{aligned}750 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 200\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0.05\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.5+0.05x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the rope will stretch if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=65$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5+0.05(65) \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the rope will stretch $3.75$ meters if a $65$-kilogram mass is hung from it.\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=65$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-139749/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139749"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-41467/selection/9a15bbf0c166886a5c4b62896a81ab7a368d48f04435e072dfa69859f54d5537" :answer/id #uuid "13c21312-ef78-5636-9ef4-eb03d19fb588" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$27.9$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-41467/selection/1b83027cf0d77b7ea1b72ce1984a8e0b71a9141831c36af1a68f0e7659f8a110" :answer/id #uuid "37c5db0c-e66d-5543-ae71-95d8e42cae2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$29.8$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-41467/selection/f2c8937656e086a607775ae08f49be705c69eb11ac59ac325cbdc136a4d16e3e" :answer/id #uuid "abef495e-2d84-5a1b-a72c-830ee2b2725b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$29$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-41467/selection/61d85ea5abf4326d46ec7496c4cc5c28213683fc065ab9b36958c0d5a88e51fd" :answer/id #uuid "4fdcf1f5-3f8c-59f2-8141-b1670b3417ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$28.5$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-41467/selection/3608a98b0331e4c35a826a5816ced13c1213d7a340cce40c00999e9d813517df" :answer/id #uuid "7b2c4175-0dad-5828-81f5-51eae679a5d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$28$ million" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-41467/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a062b0c2-57df-58cd-b63e-ffcb26cc9864" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-41467/selection/1b83027cf0d77b7ea1b72ce1984a8e0b71a9141831c36af1a68f0e7659f8a110" :answer-field/choices ["q-41467/selection/9a15bbf0c166886a5c4b62896a81ab7a368d48f04435e072dfa69859f54d5537" "q-41467/selection/1b83027cf0d77b7ea1b72ce1984a8e0b71a9141831c36af1a68f0e7659f8a110" "q-41467/selection/f2c8937656e086a607775ae08f49be705c69eb11ac59ac325cbdc136a4d16e3e" "q-41467/selection/61d85ea5abf4326d46ec7496c4cc5c28213683fc065ab9b36958c0d5a88e51fd" "q-41467/selection/3608a98b0331e4c35a826a5816ced13c1213d7a340cce40c00999e9d813517df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-41467" :question/id #uuid "e04b9559-7385-5bf2-a965-e72e3bc7ef64" :question/math-academy-id "q-41467" :question/problem "The relationship between the advertising budget and sales (both in millions of dollars) of a company per year over several years is shown in the following table. \n\n| Advertising $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.5$** | **$3$** | **$3.5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sales $(y)$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 168\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 168\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4.2 \\\\ 6.8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-4.2+6.8x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the value of the sales when the advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, we substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.2+6.8(5) \\\\  & =29.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the total sales when the advertising budget equals $$5$ million will be $$29.8$ million. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=5$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-41467/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-41467"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-139750"] :question/problem "A biologist has recorded data on a pine tree's age (in years) and height (in feet). This data is shown below. \n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Height $(y)$ | $1.2$ | $2.5$ | $3.6$ | $4.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the tree's height after $8$ years, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1.2 \\\\ 2.5 \\\\ 3.6 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}15 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11.8 \\\\ 35\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 1.1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=0.2+1.1x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the  height after $8$ years, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.2+1.1(8) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the model, the height of a pine tree that is $8$ years old will be $9$ feet. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-139735/selection/a45ef737895836fa04a646c02825ca2245d29d2c2dfbcd759ccb8d6154319453" :answer/id #uuid "5e00fa0e-9e68-5fe8-a248-f64702a9d47d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$2\\,800$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139735/selection/3013804da636fe272a4cd2736018d57974bb22e02ef90046bc52001585987604" :answer/id #uuid "628717b8-4d7c-5201-a50c-a2a14f9d925c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$4\\,400$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139735/selection/5ad997cbe1d72613935811d8685a0785b8389e045678a114282bf5e1f4d80f82" :answer/id #uuid "6c4e3e0a-6fa6-54d1-8c13-a844ab31c878" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$4\\,200$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139735/selection/a42c46c6210724cb12b4f9ffbe4a9c13e3da27746d307bff39fd958d9060e549" :answer/id #uuid "85e28c32-b691-5661-bf19-15b081857652" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$3\\,200$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139735/selection/6b6fb814d0e700beccfefd4efd51ea31e23cba28a7afcd07b66a6639cc8b789a" :answer/id #uuid "b7efd5a7-9470-5ec3-aeb2-a3ccabeee676" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$3\\,700$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-139735/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "98bcbbb1-f12c-5d23-9b4c-213292e8d0cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-139735/selection/a42c46c6210724cb12b4f9ffbe4a9c13e3da27746d307bff39fd958d9060e549" :answer-field/choices ["q-139735/selection/a45ef737895836fa04a646c02825ca2245d29d2c2dfbcd759ccb8d6154319453" "q-139735/selection/3013804da636fe272a4cd2736018d57974bb22e02ef90046bc52001585987604" "q-139735/selection/5ad997cbe1d72613935811d8685a0785b8389e045678a114282bf5e1f4d80f82" "q-139735/selection/a42c46c6210724cb12b4f9ffbe4a9c13e3da27746d307bff39fd958d9060e549" "q-139735/selection/6b6fb814d0e700beccfefd4efd51ea31e23cba28a7afcd07b66a6639cc8b789a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-139735" :question/id #uuid "d0607109-09b8-52c8-8857-0db36b038506" :question/math-academy-id "q-139735" :question/problem "A company collected data on the sale price (in thousands of dollars) of a particular used car model and the car's age (in years). This data is shown below.\n\n| Age $(x)$ | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$6$** | **$7$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Price $(y)$ | $12$ | $10$ | $8.5$ | $7$ | $6.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the sale price of a model that is $10$ years old, assuming the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}44 \\\\ 250\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 8.5 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 6.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}38 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}44 \\\\ 250\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}17.2 \\\\ -1.4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=17.2-1.4x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the selling price of a car of the same model that is $10$ years old, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =17.2-1.4(10) \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the sale price will be $$3\\,200.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=10$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-139735/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd286741-bf82-5723-a9c1-acf7a8b238c2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-139735"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9914"] :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-6$** | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-7$ | $-4$ | $2$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n\nThe vector of coefficients  of the line $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above can be found using linear regression as \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072172/assets/8002d38943a0f0b72e5c9cef2e3a455915479ca86c53ebe403b60a6b49f268a4.png)\n\nwhere $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector. Which are the design matrix $X$ and the observation vector $\\mathbf{y}$ in this context?" :question/worked-solution "| $x$ | **$\\color{blue}-6$** | **$\\color{blue}-3$** | **$\\color{blue}-1$** | **$\\color{blue}0$** | **$\\color{blue}4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $\\color{red}-7$ | $\\color{red}-4$ | $\\color{red}2$ | $\\color{red}8$ | $\\color{red}10$ |\n\nThe coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5610"] :question/problem "| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above.\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072172/assets/ba4b73a8acd1ede70da8d25a67159c74420ac2fd11d15c830a460071d881ff42.png)\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{104}[\\begin{aligned}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}27 \\\\ -26\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6.75 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $y = 6.75 - x.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5611"] :question/problem "An experiment consists of measuring how much a spring stretches (in inches) when one end of the spring is attached to the ceiling while a mass of $x$ ounces hangs from the other end. This data is shown below.\n\n| Mass $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $0.8$ | $1.8$ | $3.2$ | $4.2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the spring stretch if we use an $8$-ounce mass, assuming that the trend continues?  \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072172/assets/400621e90f0419646dcd9e2b2e1bc31bc3cc280bef2be6c3d330c8b69308429c.png)\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*" :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}0.8 \\\\ 1.8 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 4.2\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T,$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X )^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X )^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}[\\begin{aligned}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0.04 \\\\ 0.82\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y = 0.04+ 0.82x.$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the spring will stretch if an $8$-ounce mass is hung from it,  we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.04+0.82(8) \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, according to the model, if an $8$ ounce mass is hung on the spring, the spring will stretch $6.6$ inches. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
